    distinguish:
      "The Cape Peninsula coast shares the same Table Mountain Group sandstone as Table Mountain, but the two landforms diverge: Table Mountain is the same rock layer surviving nearly horizontal as a plateau under a resistant cap, while here that same layer has been carved by long wave action into steep sea cliffs. Compared with Namibia's existing Cape Cross entry — both single out coast as a distinct landform category — the Namibian coast is fog-bound and dominated by the cold Benguela Current, while this is an open headland coast where the Atlantic's broad swell breaks directly against the rock.",
