      "The Cape of Good Hope is widely believed to mark where the Atlantic and Indian Oceans meet, but this is in fact a well-documented misconception — the true southernmost point of the African continent is Cape Agulhas, about 150 km east of the Cape of Good Hope. Where the cold Benguela Current and the warm Agulhas Current actually meet shifts along the coastline somewhere between the two capes, and there is no visible dividing line in the water. Understanding this is the key to correcting the common \"two oceans meet here\" misconception.",
    history:
      "The Cape Peninsula coast has been an important landmark on the shipping routes between Europe and points east since the age of exploration, and its history includes repeated maritime incidents caused by heavy fog and offshore reefs — the name \"Cape of Good Hope\" itself carried early navigators' hope of rounding this stretch safely and reaching the Indian Ocean beyond. Today this coastline forms part of Table Mountain National Park and is managed as a protected natural area.",
